# ADR 023: LOW BOND CHURN UPDATE

## Changelog

- 31/01/2026: initial proposal

## Status

Proposed

## Context

Currently, three nodes are forced to churn out on every churn event: the oldest node, the worst-performing node, and the lowest-bonded node.

Churning out the oldest and worst-performing nodes generally improves the health of the network. The inclusion of the lowest-bonded node is intended to prevent capture of the active set while maximising bonded RUNE.

When the network is at the node cap and a higher-bonded node is waiting to churn in, removing the lowest-bonded node increases the total active bond and aligns with this intent.

However, when the network is below the node cap, removing the lowest-bonded node simply reduces the amount of active bond without improving decentralisation or security.

Since this mechanism was introduced, the network has changed substantially. Nodes are now commonly funded via external bond providers rather than entirely by operators. As a result, newer nodes typically enter with lower bond and are frequently churned out, making it harder for new operators to attract bond and become established.


## Decision

TBC

## Detailed Design

The change would be minor.
Unless the network is at the node cap, then only the oldest and worst performing nodes be churned out.
If the network reaches the node cap, the lowest bond node is churned out in addition to the oldest and worst.

## Consequences

### Positive

- Prevents unnecessary reduction of active bond when the network is below the node cap.
- Makes it easier for new operators to attract bond providers and become established.
- Maintains protection against active-set capture when the network is at capacity.

### Negative

- Established operators could crowd out the network with minimum bonded nodes.
- This would then return the network to the current behaviour.

### Neutral

